    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d146">

                <pb facs="0117" n="109" sameAs="frus:frus1939v05/pg109"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.8591/648</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Acting Liaison Officer</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Notter</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-12-19">December 19, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In a telephone call at 5:05 p.m. today the Navy Department informed that
                    instructions had been issued to the <hi rendition="#italic">Tuscaloosa</hi> that
                    when she had completed the task of rescuing the survivors from the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Columbus</hi> she was to put in to the port of New York.
                    While it is not possible as yet to say just when the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tuscaloosa</hi> will arrive in New York, it is likely that she will arrive
                    late tomorrow afternoon.</p>
                <p>Upon being questioned as to whether the <hi rendition="#italic">Tuscaloosa</hi>
                    had been released from her observation duty, I was informed that release from
                    such duty was to take place not at a given hour but at a given longitude <pb
                        facs="0118" n="110" xml:id="pg110"/> and that the calculations of the Navy
                    Department were on an approximate basis, that that point would have been reached
                    by about 1 o’clock today. It appears that instead of that longitude having been
                    reached at that hour the <hi rendition="#italic">Tuscaloosa</hi> was still
                    engaged in observation at the time when the British destroyer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hyperion</hi> came into view and the crew of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Columbus</hi> began to scuttle the ship. If the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tuscaloosa</hi> had completed her observation duty she
                    would have turned about to return to her base in Hampton Roads.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harley Notter</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
